48,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,400,89,3,387,264,6351,1372,1741,429,400,89,47822,16,18,92,284,220,16,2418,220,6771,400,86,62,16,4779,400,86,62,17,4779,57960,67816,4779,400,86,4698,3,387,678,279,3204,2750,315,198,78045,89,488,1147,61,18,488,1147,61,19,488,1147,61,24,488,1147,47822,16,15,92,488,1147,47822,16,17,92,7110,60,9885,400,86,62,16,61,17,488,289,62,17,61,17,488,1124,67816,488,289,4698,61,17,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $z$ be a complex number such that $z^{13} = 1.$  Let $w_1,$ $w_2,$ $\\dots,$ $w_k$ be all the possible values of\n\\[z + z^3 + z^4 + z^9 + z^{10} + z^{12}.\\]Find $w_1^2 + w_2^2 + \\dots + w_k^2.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"650243ec544a031ade9d40e7da94ac928a3ce47d96d0931ce78461fdbcc14701"},"seconds":45.07427406311035,"seed":697054046864959910,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve this problem, we start by noting that \\(z\\) is a 13th root of unity, which means \\(z\\) satisfies the equation \\(z^{13} = 1\\). The 13th roots of unity are given by \\(z = e^{2\\pi i k / 13}\\) for \\(k = 0, 1, 2, \\dots, 12\\). These roots can be written as \\(z = \\omega^k\\) where \\(\\omega = e^{2\\pi i / 13}\\) is a primitive 13th root of unity.\n\nWe need to find all possible values of the expression \\(z + z^3 + z^4 + z^9 + z^{10} + z^{12}\\) as \\(z\\) ranges over all 13th roots of unity. Let's denote this expression by \\(S(z)\\):\n\\[ S(z) = z + z^3 + z^4 + z^9 + z^{10} + z^{12}. \\]\n\nSince \\(z\\) is a 13th root of unity, the set of all possible values of \\(S(z)\\) is the same as the set of all possible values of \\(S(\\omega^k)\\) for \\(k = 0, 1, 2, \\dots, 12\\). To find the sum of the squares of these values, we use the fact that the sum of the squares of the values of a polynomial evaluated at the roots of unity can be computed using the properties of roots of unity and symmetry.\n\nFirst, we observe that the polynomial \\(S(z)\\) is symmetric in the roots of unity. The sum of the squares of the values of \\(S(z)\\) as \\(z\\) ranges over all 13th roots of unity is given by:\n\\[ \\sum_{k=0}^{12} S(\\omega^k)^2. \\]\n\nTo compute this, we use the fact that the sum of the squares of the values of a polynomial evaluated at the roots of unity can be expressed in terms of the coefficients of the polynomial. Specifically, if \\(P(z)\\) is a polynomial, then:\n\\[ \\sum_{k=0}^{12} P(\\omega^k)^2 = 13 \\sum_{j=0}^{12} |a_j|^2, \\]\nwhere \\(a_j\\) are the coefficients of the polynomial \\(P(z)\\).\n\nIn our case, the polynomial \\(S(z)\\) is:\n\\[ S(z) = z + z^3 + z^4 + z^9 + z^{10} + z^{12}. \\]\nThe coefficients are all 1 for the terms \\(z, z^3, z^4, z^9, z^{10}, z^{12}\\) and 0 for the other terms. Therefore, the sum of the squares of the coefficients is:\n\\[ 1^2 + 1^2 + 1^2 + 1^2 + 1^2 + 1^2 = 6. \\]\n\nThus, the sum of the squares of the values of \\(S(z)\\) as \\(z\\) ranges over all 13th roots of unity is:\n\\[ 13 \\times 6 = 78. \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{78}. \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{78}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
